 pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Game State and Strategic Analysis: A Peasant\'s Perspective**\n\nOkay, here\'s the situation. The Landlord, that scoundrel, just opened with a measly pair of 6s. I\'m sitting here as a Peasant, and my job is to disrupt the Landlord and help my teammate, who\'s to my left. My hand is stacked with pairs – 7s, 8s, 10s, Queens, and even a pair of 2s, along with a triplet of 3s, some singles, and the Red Joker.\n\nFirst things first, I need to understand my role in this counter-clockwise game flow: Landlord -> Me -> Teammate. It\'s my turn *after* the Landlord. This means I\'m the "gatekeeper," the one tasked with forcing the Landlord to play strong cards early, or help my partner in setting the board for a win.\n\nMy initial thought is, do I let my teammate handle this 6s play? No way! I have a plethora of pairs.  The Landlord is signaling this game is going to be about pairs.  I need to capitalize on this and not let this small opening go unanswered. My immediate priority is to stop the Landlord from getting a free round with a small play.\n\nSo, let\'s analyze the options. I have several pairs that can beat the 6s: 7s, 8s, 10s, Queens, and the mighty 2s.  Playing any of these will disrupt the Landlord, and I need to do that as early as possible.\n\nThe 2s are too valuable to burn now. The Queens, are a bit of a stretch and the 10s are in the middle of a perfect ladder of pairs. 8s are good to have later in the game. That leaves my lowest pair, the 7s. Playing 7s forces the Landlord to respond with at least an 8 or better, or gives my teammate, who\'s after me, a chance to take control if they have the appropriate cards. If the Landlord has something higher, then I can see what I have left.\n\nEssentially, I need to dictate the flow of the game, and my hand is well-suited for that! I\'ve got a pair-heavy hand, the Landlord started with a small pair, and I can block that by playing my lowest possible pair. I don\'t want to get into the mentality of passing and setting my teammate up, I need to take the initiative! The 7s are a perfect way to do that.\n\n**Decision:** Play the Pair of 7s (♠7 ♥7).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has opened with a Pair of 6s, which is a very low combination. As the player immediately following the Landlord (the "Gatekeeper"), my primary responsibility is to prevent the Landlord from passing small cards easily and to force out their higher cards. My hand is extremely pair-heavy (77, 88, 1010, QQ, 22), making this an ideal game type for me. Passing would allow the Landlord to potentially keep control or force my teammate to play high cards unnecessarily.\n\nReason: I have a Pair of 7s, which is the most efficient counter to the Pair of 6s. It is my lowest pair, preserving my higher pairs (88, 1010, QQ, 22) for later in the round. Playing the 7s blocks the Landlord\'s low play without wasting strong cards and signals to my teammate that I can handle pair plays.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠7", "♥7"]}'}
