r with my pairs, I am blocking my teammate who just used AA. If I play 22, I give the Landlord a break from using their 2s or Jokers.\n\nConsidering card counting, the Black Joker is out. There are two remaining 2s - my 2s and Landlord and teammate\'s. My teammate\'s recent AA suggests they may be playing for control with the pair strategy. If the Landlord had 22 or Jokers, let them play it and use those cards. I need to make the Landlord spend high cards and use their best moves. If I play 22, I use my high cards, which is bad for a Peasant. I need to save my 2s and Joker for later. So, I shouldn\'t beat my teammate\'s AA.\n\nI also need to see if I\'m blocking my teammate. If they played AA, they want to lead. If I beat them, I\'m messing up their strategy. I should only do that if my hand can run out quickly. My hand has 15 cards left, it cannot run out quickly with the mess of 333, 4, 5, 88, 1010, QQ, K, 22, RJ. I need my 22 and RJ later to catch the Landlord, not beat my teammate.\n\nLetting the Landlord use their cards is the best. The Landlord has two cards in the hand: 22 and Jokers. It\'s better to let the Landlord use those cards.\n\nLet\'s double-check. The teammate played AA. Generally, the teammate may be saying "I have the best pair, I want to lead." If they wanted to pass, they would have played small. If they wanted me to bomb, they\'d signal it, and I don\'t have a bomb. If I play 22, I am left with RJ as my only play. But, I still have 13 cards, meaning I can\'t run out.\n\nSo, I should Pass.\n\nThe provided move list is "22" or "Pass", and I should choose pass. My teammate is in control.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (Previous Player) has played a pair of Aces (♠A ♦A) to beat the Landlord\'s pair of Queens. This is a strong control move, likely intended to force out the Landlord\'s highest cards (2s or Jokers) or to win the lead. I hold a pair of 2s (♠2 ♣2) and the Red Joker, which are strong control cards. However, using my 2s to beat my teammate\'s Aces would be counterproductive for two reasons:\n1. It wastes my high cards on my ally, leaving me weaker against the Landlord later.\n2. It relieves the pressure on the Landlord, effectively "saving" them from having to use their own top cards to beat the Aces.\nSince I cannot win immediately (I have 15 cards with unconnected low cards), I should trust my teammate\'s play and conserve my strength.\n\nReason: Beating a teammate\'s high control hand (AA) with my top cards (22) is poor strategy as it wastes resources and blocks the teammate. I must let the Landlord spend cards to beat the AA or let the teammate maintain the lead.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
